            <ArticleTitle>Thoracic paravertebral block using real-time ultrasound guidance.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>248-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1213/ANE.0b013e3181c35906</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">We developed a technique for ultrasound-guided paravertebral block, which was subsequently applied in the clinical setting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">An initial cadaver study was used to develop a technique that was used in the clinical setting on patients undergoing breast cancer surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Paravertebral catheters were correctly placed in the cadaveric trial in 8 of 10 attempts. In the clinical study, all blocked patients (n = 9) had evidence of thoracic wall sensory block and analgesia postoperatively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Determined by anatomical dissection, we have described the ultrasound features of the thoracic paravertebral space and performed clinically successful ultrasound-guided paravertebral block.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
